RECORD 3 — AMENDMENT to ADR 0032's pre-registered condition
written 2026-09-02, before any correction has been fitted, holdout unspent
supersedes the "A2 above 2.492" condition in decisions/0032-calibration-form.md
=============================================================================

ADR 0032 as committed states: "if the corrected development PIT still has A2 above
2.492, the two-parameter form has not fixed the shape". That condition is wrong
twice over and is replaced here rather than at fitting time.

DEFECT 1 -- the threshold assumes independence.
2.492 is the asymptotic 5% point of A2 for a fully specified uniform null under
IID draws. The panel is 178 items in 18 occupied blocks with an intra-cluster
correlation of rho=0.276. Regenerated on the panel's own structure:

    iid table                     2.492
    moving-block bootstrap        5.084   (the machinery used for every other
                                           interval in this project)
    equicorrelated, rho=0.276    12.087

The observed uncorrected A2 is 3.044, so under either cluster-robust value the
DEVELOPMENT PIT DOES NOT DEPART FROM UNIFORM. The earlier "the shape departs"
claim was an artefact of the iid critical value, and is withdrawn. I had already
caveated the KS p-value on precisely this ground and then failed to apply it to A2.

DEFECT 2 -- the condition could not fail.
A2 is 3.044 BEFORE any correction, and a correction that improves calibration can
only reduce it. Against a threshold of 5.084 the condition would pass whatever
happened. A test that cannot fail is the same defect as a statistic that cannot
fire, and both are worse than no test because they look like one.

AND the obvious replacement is worse still: the TAIL RATIO IS INVARIANT to the
correction by construction. Under z -> (z-a)/b:
    RMS((z-a)/b) = RMS(z-a)/b        MAD-scale((z-a)/b) = MAD-scale(z)/b
so the b cancels EXACTLY. Verified numerically: b=1.30 and b=0.75 both leave the
ratio at 1.2064 to four decimals. It is not perfectly invariant to a, because RMS
is not location-invariant while MAD-scale is -- RMS(z-a)^2 = RMS(z)^2 - 2a*mean(z)
+ a^2 -- but the movement is second order (1.2046 at a=-0.05, 1.2304 at a=+0.20)
and a is predicted to be near zero. The tail ratio therefore cannot test whether
the correction worked. It measures kurtosis, and kurtosis is exactly what a
location-scale map leaves alone.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
REPLACEMENT SUCCESS CONDITION -- what a location-scale map can actually move
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  The corrected development MAD-scale of z must be consistent with 1.0, judged by
  the cluster-robust 95% CI from the same moving-block bootstrap.

  Starting value: MAD-scale(z) = 1.1052, CI [0.9307, 1.4139], which ALREADY
  covers 1.0.

  This is not circular, and the reason matters: b is fitted by minimising the
  development LOG SCORE, not by matching MAD-scale. The log score is pulled
  upward by the seven |z|>3 outcomes, so the log-score-optimal b may well exceed
  1.1052 and push the corrected body BELOW 1.0 -- over-dispersing 171 ordinary
  items to cover seven extreme ones. That is the failure mode this condition
  exists to catch, and it is a real possibility rather than a formality.

  FAILS IF the corrected CI excludes 1.0 in either direction. Under-dispersion
  means the correction did not go far enough; over-dispersion means the tails
  captured the scale, which is the specific harm ADR 0032 chose the log score to
  avoid and would mean that choice did not work.

-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
TAIL PREDICTION -- pre-committed, so the successor is not a reaction
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
  A shift and a scale cannot alter kurtosis. The corrected tail ratio is
  therefore PREDICTED TO BE UNCHANGED at 1.2064 +/- the second-order movement in
  a described above, and the |z|>3 exceedance count is predicted to fall only as
  far as rescaling moves the threshold -- not to the Gaussian expectation.

  This is a prediction, not a disappointment. If it holds, it confirms the family
  is inadequate for the tails, which was stated in ADR 0032 before any of this.

  PRE-COMMITTED SUCCESSOR: Student-t on the simulated returns, with the degrees
  of freedom fitted on development only, adopted IF AND ONLY IF the tail excess
  REPLICATES on the ambiguous band under record 2. Naming it now, with the
  trigger attached to an independent sample rather than to the size of a
  development residual, is what stops it being a third parameter reached for
  because the second one disappointed.

  If record 2 does not replicate, the tail excess is withdrawn and Student-t is
  NOT adopted, whatever the corrected development numbers look like.
